<h1>Breaking Privacy in Federated Learning</h1>
<div class="small">IA-2 &middot; Implementation, Evaluation and Technical Outcomes &middot; Suyash Humne (16014223085)</div>

<div class="abstract">
<b>Abstract.</b> Building on the IA-1 attack, this report delivers the complete
implementation and evaluation of a unified privacy benchmark for federated
learning. An honest-but-curious server reconstructs a client's private image from
a shared gradient at <b>{{HERO_PSNR}}&nbsp;dB</b>; we then measure four defence
families drawn from the five syllabus modules &mdash; Differential Privacy,
Local Differential Privacy, Compressive Privacy (sparsification and
quantization), and anonymity-based aggregation &mdash; against that same attack,
reporting the full privacy&ndash;utility trade-off. A second attack
(<b>membership inference, LiRA</b>) quantifies leakage and <b>empirically audits
the differential-privacy guarantee</b>: the undefended model is attacked at
AUC&nbsp;shown below, and DP-SGD collapses the attack's low-false-positive-rate
advantage. We additionally extend the reconstruction to small batches and
cross-check the hand-written attack against the published <i>breaching</i>
framework. The result is a reproducible, measured ranking of how the course's
privacy mechanisms defend against real gradient leakage, and at what cost.
</div>

<h2>1&nbsp;&nbsp;Problem Refinement and Changes Since IA-1</h2>
<p>The IA-1 problem statement is retained; the refinements made in response to
feasibility and preliminary results are:</p>
<ul>
<li><b>From one defence to a four-defence comparison.</b> IA-1 established the
attack; IA-2 commits to a measured comparison across DP, LDP, compressive and
anonymity defences, because the preliminary attack worked cleanly and left
compute budget for the full sweep.</li>
<li><b>Added membership inference as a second, quantitative lens.</b> Reconstruction
metrics alone do not certify a privacy <i>guarantee</i>; LiRA provides a worst-case,
per-example leakage measure and lets us audit DP empirically.</li>
<li><b>Group-consistency (restart-median) reconstruction</b> was added after
observing seed variance in IA-1.</li>
<li><b>Scope boundary clarified:</b> malicious-server attacks are treated
analytically and via cross-validation, not re-implemented, to keep the individual
implementation focused and rigorous.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Research questions</h3>
<ol>
<li>How faithfully can a shared gradient be inverted to recover a private image and label?</li>
<li>Which syllabus defence most effectively degrades the attack per unit of lost accuracy?</li>
<li>Does an empirical membership-inference audit agree with the analytical DP &epsilon;?</li>
</ol>

