<h3>5.4&nbsp;&nbsp;Critical analysis, limitations and validity threats</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Ranking.</b> Differential privacy is the only defence with a formal
guarantee; compressive defences (pruning/quantization) are cheap and effective
against <i>this</i> optimisation attack but offer no proof and are known to be
weaker against adaptive attackers. LDP buys a stronger trust model at the largest
utility cost. Anonymity-via-aggregation is necessary but, as the literature on
malicious-server attacks shows, not sufficient alone.</li>
<li><b>Validity threats.</b> Results are on a small model and low-resolution data;
absolute numbers will differ at scale, though the qualitative trade-offs are
expected to hold. Reconstruction is seed-sensitive (mitigated by restarts).
The honest-but-curious assumption understates risk relative to malicious servers.</li>
<li><b>Cross-validation.</b> The hand-written cosine+TV attack was checked for
consistency against the published <i>breaching</i> framework's Inverting-Gradients
configuration to confirm the implementation is faithful.</li>
</ul>

<h2>6&nbsp;&nbsp;Conclusions</h2>
<p>Gradient sharing is not privacy: an honest-but-curious server reconstructed a
client's private image and label from the uploaded gradient alone. Of the
syllabus defences, differential privacy provides the only formal guarantee and
measurably collapses both the reconstruction and the membership-inference
advantage, at a quantified accuracy cost; compressive defences are an efficient
practical mitigation; local DP trades utility for a stronger trust model; and
anonymity-based aggregation is necessary but insufficient. The contribution is a
single reproducible benchmark that measures all four defence families against one
attack &mdash; a comparison not previously assembled in one study.</p>

<h2>7&nbsp;&nbsp;Individual Contribution Statement</h2>
<p>All implementation, experiments, figures, and analysis are my own individual
work: the unified benchmark design, the from-scratch attack and defence code, the
privacy&ndash;utility and membership-inference evaluation protocols, the batch
extension, and this report. Published methods are cited below; the integration,
measurement, and comparison are my original contribution.</p>

